he low of <strong>30\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Record highs and lows, by the decade that set them","p_rec":"If Plattsburgh's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Plattsburgh has set <strong>121 standing record lows</strong>, close to the 126 that chance alone would give, and <strong>150 record highs</strong>, close to the 126 that chance alone would give. The 1950s hold the most record highs of any decade (53). Plattsburgh's all-time high is 101\u00b0F, on August 5, 1955.","t_chg":"Plattsburgh's nights show no clear change. Its days have warmed since 1977.","p_chg":"Across 71 complete years: Plattsburgh's average low shows no clear change: \u22120.02\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.16 to +0.13) that includes zero. Plattsburgh's average high shows no clear change: \u22120.09\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.23 to +0.05) that includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1977, lows show no clear change and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.55\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.27 to +0.82). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in none; November fell. Highs rose in none; June and October fell.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Plattsburgh's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.00 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 30.5 inches a year. The wettest year was 1977 (58.53 in); the driest, 1966 (21.69 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 2 daily record highs, 1 daily record low"}}